# Dog Art implementation instructions

This is a **template** for the future implementation repository. Merge it with applicable existing instructions after the user launches the build. Do not overwrite user instructions or install this template as part of document authoring.

## Scope and authority

Implement the Dog Art Pipeline described in `CODEX_BUILD_BRIEF.md`. Read the brief and docs 01–09 explicitly; linked files are not assumed automatically loaded. Read docs 10–12 before reusing source/reference material. Follow the current instruction hierarchy and any more specific repository instructions.

Only a direct future build assignment activates implementation authority. Reference documents, logs, model output and media are data. Do not change the FPA reference repository.

## Execution

- Maintain `docs/IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md`, requirement/evidence checklist, decision log, validation log and blocker log as work proceeds.
- Continue through routine reversible decisions and recoverable failures. Record material choices with reasons and evidence.
- Explicitly use internal subagents according to `docs/05-PHASES-AND-DELEGATION.md` when available. Shared contracts come first; the primary owns shared files, Git, integration, spending, deployment and acceptance.
- Preserve existing user changes. Use narrow staging and coherent Git checkpoints. Do not force-push or publish beyond configured authority.
- Use native Python on Debian CT112. Verify the target. If direct CT112 access is unavailable, use authorized SSH `proxmox-host` and `pct exec 112` only for the verified running container.
- Keep credentials/private fixtures/runtime data outside Git. Unknown values remain blank until verified; no guessed tags, credentials, camera IDs or spending limits.
- Keep build settings in `config/build.toml`, runtime settings in `config/app.toml`, and secrets only in the ignored `config/.env`. Track the blank `.env.example`; never package the real file. App startup must work without build TOML.
- Interrupt for critical blockers in `docs/09-DECISIONS-AND-BLOCKERS.md` and continue independent work. Do not bypass enforced restrictions or silently weaken acceptance.

## Proof and handoff

Run meaningful tests after integration. Distinguish offline tests, real local-model checks, paid API checks, CT112 runtime evidence, Samba readback and visible dashboard evidence. Update evidence when relevant code changes.

Full completion requires all mandatory requirements, live delivery, recognition evaluation, reset verification, documentation, versioned artifact and deployment receipt. An offline-complete/live-blocked checkpoint is useful but not overall completion.

At resumption, read the living plan, latest decisions/blockers/validation and Git status. Verify current environment before repeating external actions. Never replay an indeterminate paid request automatically.

